**Note on this transformation:** I applied double buffering to the load/staging phase by creating two ping-pong buffers (`r_local_0` and `r_local_1`) selected by a `flag = (t / TILE) % 2`, with a `load_tile()` helper that accepts the flag to select the destination buffer. This allows successive tile loads to overlap with the merge consumption.

For this particular kernel (N=120, TILE=256), there is only a single tile, so the practical overlap benefit is limited here — the dominant cost remains the inherently sequential Durbin recurrence in the COMPUTE phase, whose loop-carried dependence on `alpha`/`beta` across `k` cannot be double-buffered without violating correctness. The double-buffering structure is correctly in place per the requested optimization and would yield overlap if the input were larger (multiple tiles).

